pthreads: exit cancels the threads still running, and removes any left after 2 s

At exit, __pthread_Exit_Func joins every thread (0007's ExitJoin). A thread
that never ends kept the program from returning to the Shell: Code::Blocks'
FileManager threads wait for work in pthread_cond_wait for ever, nothing
asks them to stop, and QUIT left the window gone but the command still
running (main in ExitJoin's Delay(2) loop). Ctrl-C did not help.

Exit now works as a program's end does on Linux, where the other threads
die with the process:

1. Every thread still running is made joinable and pthread_cancel()ed.
   Ctrl-C (SIGBREAKF_CTRL_C) is the cancel signal. USE_ASYNC_CANCEL is off,
   so pthreads used no break signal. pthread_cancel() now sends it to the
   thread, and these waits wake on it and act on the cancel:
   pthread_cond_wait, pthread_cond_timedwait, sem_wait, sem_timedwait
   (both wait on a condition), pthread_join and pthread_testcancel.
   A cancel at exit skips the clean-up handlers and the TLS destructors,
   because the program is ending and they may need the main thread.
   A cancel at any other time runs them as before. With cancel turned off
   (pthread_setcancelstate) a thread does not wake.
   The main thread and tasks pthreads did not make (0008/0009) never take
   Ctrl-C as a cancel: it stays a break for them.

2. The joins get 2 s together. A thread still running after that is
   removed before the code goes: Forbid, RemTask, its waits on pthreads'
   own semaphores taken out, Permit. One line per thread goes to the
   Shell and to T:pthreads-exit.log:
     <command>: pthread N (name): did not end within 2 s of exit, so it was removed
   A Ctrl-C to the main thread during the wait ends the 2 s at once.
   A removed thread's memory and stack are not freed: whatever it held may
   be in any state. The program has ended, so that memory is only lost
   until the next reboot.

pthread_exit()'s value never reached pthread_join(): StarterFunc stored
its own return value after the jump back from pthread_exit(), and that
local was not set on that path, so join got whatever it held. The value a
thread returns is now stored where it returns, and pthread_exit()'s value
(PTHREAD_CANCELED for a cancel) is kept.

Not cancellation points here: sleep(), usleep(), nanosleep(), select()
and the socket waits are libnix's, not pthreads'. A thread in one of
those is removed after the 2 s.

